"""The two finding models `device-services` mints, and the widened `control_type` choices.

TWO MINTS FOR A DOMAIN THAT ALREADY SHIPPED SEVEN OF TEN CONTROLS. `integrations.DnsSettings`
carries PAN-SVC-003 and `integrations.ServiceRoute` carries PAN-SVC-006; the domain's three
other asserted subjects - NTP, SNMP and system identity - already had a type, a kind, a
generator and a compiler, and this migration adds nothing for them. Two further subjects get
nothing on purpose: `integrations.DeviceTelemetry`, whose one assertion record stage 5
discharged `engineer_answers` so PAN-SVC-008 owes no query and ships no catalog entry at all,
and `integrations.ManagementService`, which no assertion of this domain names.

THE `AlterField` CHOICES LIST IS GENERATED AND NOT HAND-WRITTEN. It is a literal that must
match `Control.ControlType` exactly, and `makemigrations --check --dry-run` is the only thing
that catches a transcription slip - so this file was produced by `makemigrations assessments
--name device_services_findings` and this docstring prepended to it.

`ServiceRouteFinding` CARRIES `subject_scope` AND `DnsSettingsFinding` DOES NOT, which is the
one asymmetry worth reading twice. The resolver settings are one row per appliance, so the
appliance IS the subject. The service paths are one row per ENFORCEMENT POINT - the
firewall-wide set, then every virtual system - so the subject name is a partition name like
`vsys3`, which is not unique in an estate, and the scope is what carries the appliance.

THE CROSS-APP DEPENDENCY IS ON A COMMITTED OptivEdgeIntegrations MIGRATION. Django writes the
dependency on the latest migration present on disk in the other app, which in this pipeline can
be one nobody has reviewed; checked by eye here - `integrations.0121_zone_dos_protection` is
committed and `git status` in that repository is clean, so a clean checkout of this repo alone
can apply this.
"""
